---

<BaseLayout
  title="Tianjin Red Facing-Heaven Chilli | Tenda Peppers"
  description="Tianjin red / facing-heaven (朝天椒) chilli — cluster-type pods at 30,000–50,000 SHU for export whole chillies and powder. China manufacturer."
  jsonLd={[productJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/products/">Products</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/products/dried-chillies/">Dried Chillies</a> &rsaquo; Tianjin Red
    </nav>
    <h1>Tianjin Red (Facing-Heaven) Chilli</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Cluster-type facing-heaven (朝天椒) chillies at roughly 30,000–50,000 SHU — the mainstream
      high-heat whole chilli and powder for export, shipped through the Tianjin trading hub.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> "Tianjin Red" is an export name, not a single botanical variety. It refers to facing-heaven (朝天椒) cluster-type pods — small, upward-pointing, roughly 30,000–50,000 SHU — the dependable high-heat workhorse for export whole chillies, powder and flakes.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What is Tianjin red chilli?</h2>
    <p>
      Tianjin Red is the name exporters use for facing-heaven (朝天椒) chillies traded through
      Tianjin, one of China's largest chilli collection and export ports. The pods are small,
      cluster-type and grow pointing upward — the feature that gives the group its name. Heat sits
      at roughly 30,000–50,000 SHU: above the medium band and just below the extra-hot Jixin and
      Xiaomi.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Heat (SHU)</strong></td><td>30,000 – 50,000 (medium-high to high)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Region</strong></td><td>Collected/exported via Tianjin</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Fruit type</strong></td><td>Cluster-type, upward-pointing pods</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Export whole, powder, flakes, segments</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="data-note">
      "Tianjin Red / facing heaven" is a trade and shape designation, not a single variety; SHU is a
      representative range (LOW–MEDIUM confidence) that varies by origin. Confirm your target SHU
      window with us.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What is Tianjin red used for?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Whole dried chillies for food service and retail</li>
      <li>Chilli powder and flakes for seasoning</li>
      <li>Chilli segments for soups and stews</li>
      <li>Hot sauce and chilli oil bases</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Facing heaven is a shape, not a heat level</h2>
    <p>
      This is the point most buyers miss. "Facing heaven" (朝天椒) describes a <strong>pod shape</strong>
      — cluster-type pods that point upward as they mature — not one botanical variety with one fixed
      heat. Across different origins, facing-heaven chillies span from roughly 30,000 to over
      50,000 SHU. So an order for "facing-heaven chilli" without an origin and a SHU window is
      underspecified: two suppliers can both ship "facing heaven" and deliver pods 20,000 SHU apart.
      If batch consistency matters, lock the origin and the SHU window — or switch to a named variety
      like Sanying.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Tianjin Red vs Sanying vs Zidantou</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Item</th><th>SHU</th><th>What it locks</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Tianjin Red / facing heaven</strong></td><td>30,000 – 50,000</td><td>Shape + heat band (trade designation)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sanying (Tianying)</strong></td><td>20,000 – 50,000</td><td>Named variety (Henan/Hebei)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Zidantou (Bullet)</strong></td><td>20,000 – 50,000</td><td>Named variety, thick flesh for powder</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Use a named variety (Sanying or Zidantou) when you need a locked, reproducible profile
      batch-to-batch. Use "Tianjin red / facing heaven" when you want a general high-heat
      facing-heaven spec and are less concerned with pinning a single origin.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What most guides skip about Tianjin red</h2>
    <p>
      Most guides present facing-heaven chillies as one fixed heat level. What they skip is that the
      <strong>name describes geography and shape, not genetics</strong> — so the same label covers a
      20,000-SHU heat spread. The actionable takeaway: treat "Tianjin red" as a category you then
      tighten with an origin and a SHU window, exactly as you would tighten "red wine" with a grape
      and a region.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How to specify Tianjin red</h2>
    <p>
      Name the form (whole, powder, flakes or segments), the target SHU window, origin if known, mesh
      (for powder, 60–80 standard) and moisture (under 13%). Because the label is broad, the SHU
      window is what actually pins the product down.
    </p>

