    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is Tianjin red / facing-heaven chilli?</h3>
      <p>Tianjin Red is the export name for cluster-type facing-heaven (朝天椒) chillies shipped through Tianjin — small upward-pointing pods at roughly 30,000–50,000 SHU, the mainstream high-heat whole chilli and powder for export.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>How hot is Tianjin red / facing-heaven chilli?</h3>
      <p>Roughly 30,000–50,000 SHU — above the medium band and just below the extra-hot Jixin and Xiaomi (50,000–70,000). Because 'facing heaven' is a shape, exact SHU depends on origin.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Is facing heaven a variety or a shape?</h3>
      <p>A shape. 'Facing heaven' (朝天椒) describes cluster-type upward-pointing pods, not one variety. Heat spans 30,000–50,000+ SHU across origins, so a 'facing heaven' order without origin and SHU is underspecified.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is Tianjin red used for?</h3>
      <p>An export workhorse: whole dried chillies, chilli powder, flakes and segments for hot sauce, seasoning and food service. Its consistent medium-high heat and compact pod make it a dependable general-purpose high-heat chilli.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Tianjin red vs Sanying — what is the difference?</h3>
      <p>Both are facing-heaven-style high-heat chillies. Sanying is the named regional variety (Henan/Hebei); 'Tianjin red' is a trade/export designation. Specify Sanying for a locked variety, Tianjin red for a general spec.</p>
    </div>

